THOUGHT: NullAway complains because `cr` is declared @Nullable (implicitly) and later assigned to the @NonNull `elements` field. Although the logic ensures `elements` never becomes null, NullAway doesn’t follow the control flow. I should adjust the final compaction block so that `elements` is only ever assigned clearly non-null arrays, without relying on a ternary involving `cr`. A simple fix is to keep the `ci == 0` and `cr != null && ci != cr.length` branches, and in the final `else` split into two explicit branches: one for `cr == null` (assign new empty array) and one for `cr != null` (assign `cr`). This preserves behavior and makes the non-null guarantee obvious to NullAway. After editing, I’ll run the Gradle compile to confirm the NullAway error count drops by one and no new errors appear.

```bash
cd /home/vscode/nullness-benchmarks/wala-util && sed -i '278,290c\	    // now compact cr to "just enough"
	    size = ci;
	    if (ci == 0) {
	      elements = new int[0];
	    } else if (cr != null && ci != cr.length) {
	      int[] result = new int[ci];
	      System.arraycopy(cr, 0, result, 0, ci);
	      elements = result;
	    } else if (cr == null) {
	      // no intersection elements were collected; use empty array
	      elements = new int[0];
	    } else {
	      // cr is non-null and fully utilized
	      elements = cr;
	    }
	    return;' src/main/java/com/ibm/wala/util/intset/MutableSparseIntSet.java && ./gradlew compileJava -x test -q > new_build_output.log 2>&1 && grep -E "NullAway|error\)" new_build_output.log | tail -n 20```

